  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An anomalously strong upper level ridge is \nforecast to spread east from the OH/TN Valleys into the \nCarolinas/Mid- Atlantic Region this week. Meanwhile, anomalous \nlow-level thicknesses beneath the ridge, plus strong heating and\npersistent southerly flow, point towards the potential for \nunusually hot conditions for the area. In fact, some of our more\nreliable forecast aids point towards this being an outlier type\nof event from a climatology standpoint, with record highs \npossible. \n\nThe heat is forecast to peak Wednesday through Friday. However,\nthere are some caveats worth pointing out. There is the \npotential for a weak front or convective outflow to dip south \ninto our northern counties on Wednesday. Should this occur, a \nnot-as-hot easterly onshore flow may hold highs down some around\nthe Albemarle Sound and Northern OBX vicinity. One other \npotential caveat is thunderstorms. With subsidence aloft, the \nseabreeze should be muted this week. However, any convection \nthat can develop upstream would have a tendency to propagate \ntowards ENC thanks to the west/northwest flow aloft. Any \nconvection that survives into the area could hold temps down \nsome, especially with any afternoon convection. For now, these \nare not expected to be significant limiting factors, and even if\nso, it would most likely only impact a relatively smaller area \nof ENC. \n\nIn light of this, confidence is high regarding anomalous heat \nand humidity, with 2-3 days of significant heat impacts \nexpected. In fact, the NWS' experimental heat risk guidance \nshows most of ENC in the \"Major\" to \"Extreme\" category for heat.\n\n\nRegarding messaging, a Heat Advisory has been issued for most \nof ENC for Wednesday. For now, we have left out the central and \nnorthern OBX in light of the potential for a brief period of \neasterly flow to develop which would keep temps down some. \nAdditional heat headlines are likely through the week. As was \nmentioned yesterday, it's less common for heat headlines to be \nissued in the month of September in our forecast area. The Heat \nAdvisory for tomorrow is the first heat headline issued in \nSeptember since 2020. Additionally, please see the \"CLIMATE\" \nsection below for record information at select sites through \nFriday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong cold front is forecast to encroach on \nthe Carolinas late Friday, then push fully through ENC on \nSaturday. Ahead of the front, guidance is mixed on whether or \nnot convection will manage to develop ahead of the front over, \nor upstream of, ENC Friday afternoon and evening. Upper level \nridging may hold on one more day, but the signal in some \nguidance can't be ignored. In light of this, the forecast will \nshow modest thunderstorm probabilities Friday afternoon and \nevening (30-50% chance). The synoptic setup appears fairly \nfavorable for strong to severe thunderstorms on Friday if \nridging doesn't limit deep convection. Most machine learning and\nprobabilistic guidance favor this potential as well, and SPC \nhas outlooked a portion of the area in a Slight Risk. The risk \nof convection continues on Saturday, but the evolution may hinge\non Friday's convection.\n\nBehind Saturday's front, highs are forecast to get bumped down \nto the 80s, with lows in the 60s. This will be a notable drop \ncompared to the heat of the next few days. It may not end up \nquite as dry as some guidance previously forecast, but dewpoints\nin the 60s won't feel as oppressive as the next few days.",
